dmabuf scanout for the EGL offscreen platform (wsh-pve, LGPL-2.1 like OpenGLide)

With the EGL offscreen platform (0002-egl-offscreen.patch) qemu-3dfx reads every Glide frame back
to the CPU (getOffscreenFrame) and copies it into the VM's console. When QEMU's display has GL
(-display egl-headless, -display dbus,gl=on) it can take the frame as a dmabuf instead, a buffer
both EGL displays share, with no copy through system memory:

- getOffscreenDmabufAt(i, ...) creates scanout buffer i (0 or 1), a renderbuffer of the window's
  size, and exports it once as a dmabuf through EGL_MESA_image_dma_buf_export (fd, stride, offset,
  fourcc, modifier; the caller closes the fd). Mesa's llvmpipe exports through /dev/udmabuf, a GPU
  driver through its render node; without the extension it returns 0 and says why.
- getOffscreenScanoutAt(i) blits the front buffer into buffer i (unscaled and unflipped, which
  llvmpipe does as a memory copy; the rows stay bottom up, which the host program tells its
  display), and finishes the GL work (an EGL fence waited on, EGL_KHR_fence_sync, else glFinish),
  so the display can read it from its own context and thread at once. Buffers of their own, so that
  the next swap can't change a frame the display still reads; two, so that the host program can
  copy the next frame into one while its display still reads the other (double buffering, since
  wsh-openglide 0.8). getOffscreenDmabuf() and getOffscreenScanout() are buffer 0, as before.
- While a gamma ramp is set (grGammaCorrectionValue, grLoadGammaTable) the copy applies it, in the
  same rows: a context of its own that shares OpenGLide's objects (so none of OpenGLide's state is
  touched) copies the front buffer into a texture and draws it into the scanout buffer through a
  256x1 lookup texture (a GLSL 1.20 shader, the same lookup getOffscreenFrame applies on the CPU).
  Only if that pass can't be had (no context, no shader) does getOffscreenScanout() refuse (returns
  0) a frame with a ramp: the caller then reads it back with getOffscreenFrame().
- On a GPU the gamma pass's context waits for OpenGLide's frame in its own GL stream
  (EGL_KHR_wait_sync, a fence flushed in OpenGLide's context) instead of the CPU waiting for it;
  not with Mesa's software renderers, which don't all wait in the stream. QEMU_3DFX_WAIT_SYNC=1
  or 0 in the environment decides instead. The copy itself is always finished on the CPU, as the
  display reads it from a context of its own.

The scanout buffers go with the window (EGLFinaliseWindow). Nothing changes for a host program
that doesn't call the new exports.

No eglTerminate when the window closes any more: an EGL platform display is one handle per process,
and QEMU's own egl-headless or dbus,gl=on display on Mesa's surfaceless platform (and qemu-3dfx's
MESA GL pass-through) use the same one; terminating it destroyed their contexts (QEMU then ended in
libepoxy, "Couldn't find current GLX or EGL context").

For wsh-pve (packages/wsh-openglide), against kjliew/qemu-xtra at the commit upstream.env pins,
after 0002-egl-offscreen.patch.
---
diff --git a/openglide/platform/linux/egl.cpp b/openglide/platform/linux/egl.cpp
